{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact S1 F1 head ebd615fd99c766e356e87faa6b3b74098150a94f on builder/state-s1-f1-20261108, tree 4430c234, all six paths, for request 089ed1a1 under promise 4c2c799b and review request 99918cc7. The head fixes my S1 F1 finding: a detached layer that closes again keeps its session row. But the new early return in `#close` adds a defect, F2 below, that blocks this head. My early note 24365d0e describes the same defect.\n\nF2 (blocks). At the head, `#close` returns `owner.disposed()` for a closed owner before it reaches `done.finally(...)`. That `finally` is the only code that removes a layer from `#layers`. An OAuth generation can close itself. Its lifetime timer and `expireIfDue` call `graceful()` directly, and hosted.ts line 712 sets `lifetime.expires` from the OAuth deadline. After that, its layer stays in `#layers`:\n- On main c85553da, every later `#close` of a closed layer removes its entry: the `\"existing\"` branch in `open()`, the stale-layer loop in `open()`, `closeGrant` and `closeAll`.\n- At the head, no `#close` path removes it. Only `open()` for the same grant id clears it, by overwriting the entry when a new deadline exists, or `abrupt()`. If `standingDeadline` returns null, `open()` now returns null and leaves the stale entry, where main removed it.\n\nI ran a probe outside the builder's worktree. It bundled main's and the head's packages/mcp/src with esbuild 0.28.1 from the shared node_modules, with stub access ports:\n1. Eight grants open, every generation retires itself with `graceful()`, the eight grants stop being current, and a ninth grant opens. Main opens the ninth grant. The head returns null, because the eight stale layers still fill `OAUTH_GRANTS_MOST`. After `closeGrant` and `closeAll`, the ninth grant is still refused.\n2. Detach, then close in the same turn: main ends with `retained` false, which is the S1 F1 defect. The head ends with `retained` true.\n\nHosted MCP is closed on all three Workers